CANDIDATE DISPOSITION (the three known gaps, each checked, not restated):
  a) claim/send not atomic -> REAL LIMIT but NOT the proposal. Documented in
     app/product_service.py:311-360 and analysis/next-moves.md as R3-approved.
     Closing it needs a real atomic op (single-process lock / transactional
     send), which is bigger and riskier than what I propose.
  b) 750 unresolved spec refs / 24.2% traceability / 0% e2e tree -> REAL but
     NOT the proposal. Measured today: 480/1568 (30.6%) marker-complete,
     up from the 24.0% pinned at f74abf9. It is a ~700-file mechanical grind
     with no user-visible defect, and gate 10 already ratchets on the measured
     value. Command: python3 scripts/count_traceability_coverage.py (output below).
  c) claim whose send never completes is permanent (no TTL, no reaper) ->
     REAL WORK and IS the proposal (item 1). Reproduced live today (output below).

1. THE ONE CHANGE: give the price_alert_sent claim a TTL with expiry-on-conflict
   (plus a startup reaper), so a process killed between the claim INSERT and the
   SMTP send stops suppressing that household's price-hike alert forever.
   If this is judged too big for one slice, the minimal sub-slice is
   expiry-on-conflict alone (no reaper): on IntegrityError, read the existing
   row's notified_at, and if older than N (e.g. 24h), DELETE + re-INSERT instead
   of returning False. Nothing else.

2. FILES IT TOUCHES (exact paths) and line-level shape:
   - app/product_service.py:90-95 (price_alert_sent schema; notified_at already
     exists, no schema change needed) ; :311-360 claim_price_alert_sent (on
     IntegrityError, SELECT notified_at for the key; if now-age > TTL, DELETE
     the stale row and retry the INSERT once, else return False) ; :399-420
     release_price_alert_sent (unchanged, reused by the reaper path).
   - app/subscription_alerts.py:693-730 _claim/_release wrappers (pass through
     the new "stale-claim-stolen" outcome; no change to the send path
     :1004-1030, which already releases on every caught failure).
   - tests/test_price_alert_claim_ttl.py (NEW, one file): kill-between-claim-
     and-send repro, stale-claim re-claimable after TTL, fresh-claim still
     suppresses before TTL.
   Shape: ~30 lines in product_service.py (SELECT-age-compare-DELETE-retry),
   ~10 lines for a startup reaper (DELETE WHERE notified_at < now-TTL, called
   once at subscription-alert run start), ~60 lines of tests. No signature
   changes to public callers.

3. EVIDENCE IT IS REAL TODAY (pasted command output, not description):
   3a. Live stranded-claim reproduction (run just now, :memory: DB):
   ```
   run1 claim won: True
   run2 claim won: False -> alert SUPPRESSED forever, no TTL, no reaper
   ```
   Command: python3 -c "from app.product_service import ProductService; s =
   ProductService(':memory:'); s.claim_price_alert_sent('t1','Netflix',1999,
   '2026-10'); print(...); s.claim_price_alert_sent('t1','Netflix',1999,
   '2026-10')" -- run 1 returns True (claim taken, process 'dies' before send,
   no release), run 2 returns False (next cron tick suppresses). There is no
   third state and no path back except manual DELETE.
   3b. notified_at is write-only -- nothing can age out a claim:
   ```
   ===notified_at readers===
   app/product_service.py:94:              notified_at TEXT NOT NULL,
   app/product_service.py:303:                    "(tenant_id,merchant,amount_cents,period_ym,notified_at) "
   app/product_service.py:320:        ``notified_at`` is written here and read nowhere in ``app/`` — there
   ```
   (full grep -rn "notified_at" app/ --include="*.py" returns only the schema,
   the INSERT, and docstring mentions at product_service.py:356 and
   subscription_alerts.py:744 -- both docstrings SAYING there is no TTL.)
   Command: grep -rn "notified_at" --include="*.py" app/
   3c. No TTL/reaper anywhere near this feature:
   ```
   grep -rni "ttl\|reaper\|expire" --include="*.py" app/ src/
   ```
   returned only unrelated hits (insight_api session expiry, warranty EXPIRED
   state, platform.py idempotency 24h expiry, connection_service OAuth STATE_TTL)
   -- zero hits in product_service.py or subscription_alerts.py. The docstring
   itself confesses it (app/product_service.py:320-360): "A claim that is never
   followed by a successful send is PERMANENT... Recovery is a manual DELETE."
   3d. The crash window is structural, not hypothetical: the claim INSERT
   (subscription_alerts.py:948) precedes send_email_notification (:1004) with
   release only on CAUGHT (OSError, RuntimeError, sqlite3.Error) paths
   (:1016-1030). A kill -9 / crash / power loss between :948 and :1004 takes no
   release path. Command: sed -n '1000,1040p' app/subscription_alerts.py
   (pasted in working notes; the except clause covers only catchable errors).
   3e. Traceability re-measured today for the item-4 comparison:
